{#
  The permanent address.

  §5: "/oal/v1.0 is a permanent address. It is printed on scorecards, in the changelog,
  and in the licence line. It must resolve, unchanged, indefinitely." §13 judges the
  build on whether it "renders identically in 2032, styled by 2026's stylesheet".

  ── What "unchanged" covers, and what it deliberately does not ──────────────────────

  The published DOCUMENT is not frozen. Its CONTENT is, and so is the way that content
  renders. `assetBase` points the stylesheet, the fonts and the favicon at version-scoped
  copies taken at publication, and a colour change on the live site in 2028 cannot reach
  them — that is R2, and it is what §13's 2032 test is actually about.

  The chrome around it — masthead, version status, footer — renders live, from the same
  templates and the same data as every other page. That is R1, and until 2026-08-12 it was
  false: the whole `index.html` was stored, so this page carried the footer it was
  published with while the rest of the site had moved on. One site, two footers.

  ── Why the branch is here and not in the layout ────────────────────────────────────

  For a frozen version this template does not render `rubric.njk` at all. That is the
  point of putting the branch here: rubric prose has no path onto a frozen page, so the
  freeze holds by construction rather than by a later overwrite that has to be remembered.
  The previous arrangement rendered the page and then replaced the file, which made
  `requirePublishableVersion` a guard over discarded output and let a regeneration recipe
  re-record the old bytes and report success (CHANGES.md row 43).

  `versionOf` is what tells the layout which version this page is, so it can emit the
  stored fragment inside `<main>` with no surrounding whitespace — the `<main>` content is
  then byte-identical to what was published. Check 21 re-runs that comparison on every
  commit, and also re-runs the substring claim against the retained published document.
  It is a string rather than the record itself because eleventyComputed renders through
  Nunjucks, which would autoescape a fragment of HTML into visible markup.

  Paginating over oal.versions is what makes §10's "additive by construction" true:
  publishing v1.1 adds a record to oal.json and a directory appears.
#}
{%- if not (v.version | isFrozen) %}
{% include "rubric.njk" %}
{%- endif %}
